WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.403 --> 00:00:05.820
ABBY CAREY: Hey,
I'm Abby, and I'm

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:09.720
a developer advocate for Google
Cloud Developer Experiences.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.060
NAFIUL ISLAM: And I'm
Nafiul from JetBrains.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:14.490
And we're going to
walk you through how

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:18.300
to build a Cloud Run service
using a little plugin

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:21.450
in PyCharm called Cloud Code.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:22.890
ABBY CAREY: Cloud
Code is a plugin

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:24.900
that makes your local
development experience

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.260
with Google Cloud Services,
like GKE and Cloud Run,

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.390
more streamlined and convenient.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:32.650
It's supported for
a variety of IDEs,

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:34.490
including PyCharm and IntelliJ.

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:38.820
Nafiul, how can everyone get in
on the PyCharm and Cloud Code

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:39.660
action?

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:41.910
NAFIUL ISLAM: Well, first,
you need PyCharm installed.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:45.240
Now to install Cloud Code,
head to the marketplace

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.880
by clicking Preferences,
then Plugins,

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.860
and search for Cloud Code.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:55.060
You might be prompted
to restart PyCharm.

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:59.390
Once that's done, Cloud
Code should be ready.

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:02.020
You'll need Docker installed
and running in the background

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:03.460
to use Cloud Code.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:05.800
While Cloud Code
automatically installs

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.490
command-line dependencies,
like the Cloud SDK, for you,

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:11.680
you might need to
manually install it

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:15.220
if you have certain permissions
blocking the install.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:16.960
Later in this
video, I'll show you

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:21.160
how to add a Cloud SQL database
connection to both your Cloud

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.860
Run service and
PyCharm's database panel.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:27.490
For that step, you'll need
to install Cloud SQL Proxy.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:30.760
Check the description for
more info on how to do this.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:33.490
Abby, want to show how to
get started with a Cloud Run

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:34.150
sample?

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:35.170
ABBY CAREY: Sure thing.

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:37.300
Once you install
Cloud Code, you'll

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.880
notice some new UI in PyCharm.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:43.120
To the right, there are
Cloud Code-specific panels

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.730
that align with different
Google Cloud flows.

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:49.810
We'll be focusing on Cloud Run,
so I'll open Cloud Code's Cloud

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.190
Run panel.

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:54.550
Here, you can view your
deployed Cloud Run services

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:58.860
and right click them
to view their URLs.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:02.670
Getting started with Cloud Run
has a slight learning curve

00:02:02.670 --> 00:02:05.010
if you choose to build
your image with Docker.

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.950
With Cloud Code, you can
create a new Cloud Run

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:10.560
project that comes with
a handy Dockerfile,

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.570
or build with
buildpacks instead.

00:02:13.570 --> 00:02:18.170
To create a new project,
select New Project,

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:23.380
go over to Cloud Code, Cloud
Run, and then give it a name.

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:25.860
You can then choose
between Flask or Django

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.000
for your project.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.560
We're going to choose
Flask and hit Create.

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.570
Now that the sample
is loaded, you

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:36.390
have a boilerplate
project for Flask.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:38.910
It's a simple Hello
World service,

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:42.560
and here's that
Dockerfile I mentioned.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:44.630
To run a Cloud Run
service locally,

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:48.440
Cloud Code comes packaged
with a Cloud Run emulator.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:50.960
When you run your service
locally in the emulator,

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:53.270
you get to test
and debug your code

00:02:53.270 --> 00:02:55.410
in a production-like
environment.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.010
This sample's emulator
is already configured,

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:00.590
but what if you wanted to
add environment variables

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:04.220
or connect this service
to a Cloud SQL database?

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:08.300
You can edit your configuration,
and under Advanced Revision

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:12.620
Settings, you can update your
service's CPU allocation,

00:03:12.620 --> 00:03:17.030
Environment Variables, and
specify Cloud SQL database

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:18.440
Connections.

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:21.020
To connect your Cloud Run
service to a Cloud SQL

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:24.530
database, just put your
instance connection name here

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:25.610
and you're good to go.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:27.890
In a minute, Nafiul
will walk through how

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:30.410
to connect PyCharm to
a Cloud SQL database

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:32.930
to make working with
databases easier.

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:35.220
You can also specify
a Service Account,

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:38.000
which is very important for
authenticating your Cloud Run

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.640
service with Google Cloud APIs.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.980
And towards the bottom
of my run configuration,

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:45.480
I can pick my builder.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.260
I'll stick with
using my Dockerfile,

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:49.830
but if I didn't have one,
I could select Buildpacks,

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:52.230
which analyzes your
application structure

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:56.010
and determines the best
way to containerize it.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.140
But everything is looking good.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:03.150
Now I can either debug or run
my service in the emulator.

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:06.060
I'm only going to run this
service in this video.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:09.660
But if I were to debug
this, it would look exactly

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.750
like the PyCharm debugging
experience you're used to.

00:04:12.750 --> 00:04:15.060
All right, now let's run this.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:17.820
PyCharm is now running
the application locally.

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:19.829
The first time you
spin up the emulator

00:04:19.829 --> 00:04:23.670
can take a couple minutes, but
future runs are much faster.

00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:27.780
Now we got a local URL, so
let's try clicking on it.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.900
And boom, your app
is hosted locally

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:33.270
in the Cloud Run
emulator and logs

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:35.220
are displayed in the output.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:38.070
This service is ready
to deploy to Cloud Run.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:41.370
But before we do
that, though, Nafiul,

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:44.520
could you talk about
PyCharm's database support?

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:45.450
NAFIUL ISLAM: Yeah.

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:48.150
PyCharm Pro comes with
integrated database tools.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:51.330
PyCharm's Databases panel
allows you to seamlessly check

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.080
your Cloud SQL database.

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:57.090
This lets you check to see if
your code is making the desired

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:58.980
changes in your database.

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:02.250
To connect to a Cloud SQL
database from PyCharm,

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:05.400
you'll need to install
and run the Cloud SQL

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:06.930
Proxy from a terminal.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:10.530
Then all we need
to do is give it

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:13.620
the name of the instance
we want to connect to

00:05:13.620 --> 00:05:16.740
and the port we want to
forward that connection to.

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:20.460
This creates a proxy to
our localhost on that port.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:23.550
After doing this, we can
head into our Databases tab

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:25.380
and add a database.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:29.040
In this case, I'm
adding Postgres.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:32.580
I've setup a username and
password, so I'll put that in.

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:35.100
Now I'll test the connection.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:38.940
In general, it's always good to
check that your connection is

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:41.910
working with the configurations
and credentials you've

00:05:41.910 --> 00:05:42.840
given it.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:47.460
Once we have that
setup, we can see

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:52.030
what schemas are
available to us and what

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:54.475
tables are available in
that particular schema.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:01.530
That's the quick and easy way
to work with Cloud SQL databases

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:02.310
from PyCharm.

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:06.402
Now I think it's time
to deploy our service.

00:06:06.402 --> 00:06:07.860
ABBY CAREY: To
deploy your service,

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:11.220
you now need to edit the
Deploy Configurations

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:13.500
and give it a Google
Cloud project.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:16.230
From here, you can set
extra configurations,

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:17.790
like we did for the emulator.

00:06:17.790 --> 00:06:19.800
Everything seems to be in order.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.960
I'm building my image
locally with Docker,

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:25.080
but you can also build in
the Cloud with Cloud Build.

00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:27.660
Because we're building
this from a Dockerfile,

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:30.270
we can hit Apply and then OK.

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:33.390
Now we can press that green
Play button and run this.

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:36.720
This tells PyCharm
to start deploying.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.680
Cloud Code is now building and
pushing my container image.

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:43.450
And once that's complete,
we get a service

00:06:43.450 --> 00:06:48.150
URL and my-first-service
appears in the Cloud Run panel.

00:06:50.860 --> 00:06:55.900
We also get a handy link to the
logs of my deployed service.

00:06:55.900 --> 00:06:58.120
And congratulations,
your service

00:06:58.120 --> 00:07:00.190
is now running on Google Cloud.

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:02.970
Anyone can access it.

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:05.970
That was a look at getting
started with Cloud Run using

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:07.650
PyCharm and Cloud Code.

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:10.170
I encourage you to install
Cloud Code from your IDE's

00:07:10.170 --> 00:07:12.310
marketplace, and if
you have any questions,

00:07:12.310 --> 00:07:14.335
you can reach out to us
on our Slack channel.

00:07:14.335 --> 00:07:16.210
NAFIUL ISLAM: Thank you
so much for watching.

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:19.390
If you want to learn more
about how to use PyCharm,

00:07:19.390 --> 00:07:20.640
check out our YouTube channel.

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:22.740
We're constantly
putting out new videos

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:24.390
that will teach you
how to use PyCharm

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:27.050
and also how we make PyCharm.

